WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.240
The word agent gets stuck on almost

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:06.960
anything,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.120
from a chatbot that answers questions to

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.410
a system that deploys code to production.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.180
It's worth being precise,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.280
because the difference changes how you work

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.130
with them.

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:17.568
Think of a spectrum.

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:18.558
At one end,

00:00:18.558 --> 00:00:20.948
autocomplete suggests the next few tokens.

00:00:21.058 --> 00:00:23.058
A copilot writes bigger blocks,

00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:24.568
but only when you ask.

00:00:24.578 --> 00:00:25.998
Then comes the shift.

00:00:25.998 --> 00:00:28.038
A tool using agent acts.

00:00:28.038 --> 00:00:29.308
It reads files,

00:00:29.338 --> 00:00:30.428
runs commands,

00:00:30.498 --> 00:00:31.558
checks the result,

00:00:31.558 --> 00:00:32.788
and goes again.

00:00:32.858 --> 00:00:34.298
At the far end,

00:00:34.298 --> 00:00:36.538
autonomous agents take a goal and come

00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:37.708
back with a pull request.

00:00:38.588 --> 00:00:40.448
Three things make something agentic.

00:00:40.448 --> 00:00:40.928
Planning,

00:00:40.928 --> 00:00:42.658
where a goal becomes steps.

00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:43.528
Tool use,

00:00:43.528 --> 00:00:45.998
where every result shapes the next decision.

00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:47.178
And iteration:

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:48.108
try,

00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:48.828
observe,

00:00:48.828 --> 00:00:49.448
adjust,

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:50.318
and try again.

00:00:50.328 --> 00:00:52.408
A chatbot gives you one answer.

00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:54.158
An agent gives you a cycle.

00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:55.558
The tools are the promotion.

00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:57.532
Strip away the branding,

00:00:57.532 --> 00:01:00.192
and every coding agent has four parts.

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:00.772
A model,

00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:02.912
which reads text and writes text,

00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:04.192
and nothing else.

00:01:04.192 --> 00:01:05.992
Tools it's allowed to ask for.

00:01:05.992 --> 00:01:07.552
A loop that keeps calling the model

00:01:07.552 --> 00:01:09.272
and feeding the results back.

00:01:09.272 --> 00:01:10.332
And an environment,

00:01:10.332 --> 00:01:12.882
where the tools actually run.

00:01:13.664 --> 00:01:15.004
Here's the part people miss.

00:01:15.004 --> 00:01:17.154
The model never executes anything.

00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:19.664
A tool call is just structured text.

00:01:19.664 --> 00:01:21.604
The model asks to run the tests.

00:01:21.604 --> 00:01:23.704
The harness checks whether that's allowed,

00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:24.424
runs them,

00:01:24.424 --> 00:01:25.924
and hands the output back.

00:01:25.924 --> 00:01:27.064
The model reads it,

00:01:27.064 --> 00:01:28.744
and decides what to do next.

00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:29.994
That's the whole trick.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:32.985
That surrounding program is the harness.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:34.215
Claude Code,

00:01:34.215 --> 00:01:34.835
Codex,

00:01:34.835 --> 00:01:37.045
and Cursor are all harnesses.

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:39.105
The harness decides permissions,

00:01:39.175 --> 00:01:40.745
what stays in context,

00:01:40.835 --> 00:01:41.655
when to stop,

00:01:41.655 --> 00:01:43.015
and where the tools run.

00:01:43.015 --> 00:01:45.265
So when an agent does something reckless,

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:46.585
ask two questions.

00:01:46.595 --> 00:01:48.315
Why did the model suggest it?

00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:50.225
And why did the harness allow it?

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.095
The first,

00:01:51.095 --> 00:01:52.225
you can influence.

00:01:52.295 --> 00:01:53.095
The second,

00:01:53.095 --> 00:01:53.945
you control.

00:01:54.746 --> 00:01:55.976
And stay clear -eyed.

00:01:56.026 --> 00:01:57.416
Agents aren't magic.

00:01:57.446 --> 00:01:58.896
They hallucinate APIs,

00:01:59.146 --> 00:02:00.526
drift beyond the task,

00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:01.966
declare victory too early,

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:03.916
and lose track on long sessions.

00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:05.746
Every one of those failures has a

00:02:05.746 --> 00:02:06.246
mitigation,

00:02:06.246 --> 00:02:08.386
and those mitigations are the rest of

00:02:08.386 --> 00:02:09.236
this book.

00:02:09.836 --> 00:02:12.106
The book's mental model is Rain Man.

00:02:12.396 --> 00:02:14.286
The agent is Raymond.

00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:16.816
It counts cards faster than any human,

00:02:16.816 --> 00:02:18.416
for as long as you like.

00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:19.456
You're Charlie.

00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:21.106
You decide which casino,

00:02:21.116 --> 00:02:22.156
how much to bet,

00:02:22.156 --> 00:02:23.536
and when to cash out.

00:02:23.536 --> 00:02:25.666
That part is still your job.

00:02:25.836 --> 00:02:26.726
Next up:

00:02:26.756 --> 00:02:27.596
context,

00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:30.586
the most important skill in agentic engineering.
